@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Introduction to Azure Repos";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Azure Repos provides version control for your source code, allowing teams to collaborate efficiently while ensuring code integrity, security, and history tracking. It supports **both Git and Team Foundation Version Control (TFVC)**, making it a flexible solution for modern development teams.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is Azure Repos?</h2>
        <p>Azure Repos is a set of **cloud-hosted version control tools** that enable teams to track changes, collaborate on code, and manage repositories effectively. Version control is essential for software development, as it allows developers to work on code independently while maintaining a clean and structured codebase.</p>
        <p>With Azure Repos, teams can:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Store and manage code in private or public repositories.</li>
            <li>Track changes and maintain a history of modifications.</li>
            <li>Collaborate using pull requests and code reviews.</li>
            <li>Enforce branch policies to ensure quality and security.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>1. Git and TFVC Support</h4>
            <p>Azure Repos offers two types of version control:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Git:</strong> A distributed version control system where developers can work on their own copies of the code and merge changes later.</li>
                <li><strong>TFVC (Team Foundation Version Control):</strong> A centralized version control system where changes are committed directly to a central repository.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Git is more commonly used due to its flexibility, while TFVC is suited for enterprises with large monolithic applications.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/repos-overview.png" alt="Azure Repos Overview" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Azure Repos provides cloud-hosted Git and TFVC repositories.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-branching.png" alt="Git Branching in Azure Repos" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Branching strategies in Git repositories.</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>2. Branching and Merging</h4>
            <p>Branching allows developers to work on new features or bug fixes independently without affecting the main codebase. Popular branching strategies include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Feature Branching:</strong> Each new feature is developed in its own branch and merged once complete.</li>
                <li><strong>GitFlow:</strong> A structured workflow that includes feature, release, and hotfix branches.</li>
                <li><strong>Trunk-Based Development:</strong> All developers work on a single branch with short-lived feature branches.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>3. Pull Requests and Code Reviews</h4>
            <p>Pull requests (PRs) provide a way for developers to submit code changes for review before merging them into the main branch. Benefits include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Ensuring high-quality code through team reviews.</li>
                <li>Facilitating collaboration by allowing discussions on changes.</li>
                <li>Integrating automated build and test checks before merging.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/pull-request.png" alt="Pull Requests in Azure Repos" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Pull requests facilitate team collaboration and code reviews.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/repo-policy.png" alt="Branch Policies in Azure Repos" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Applying branch policies ensures code quality and security.</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>4. Branch Policies</h4>
            <p>Branch policies help enforce rules that ensure high-quality, secure code:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Require a minimum number of approvals:</strong> Ensures at least one reviewer approves changes before merging.</li>
                <li><strong>Enforce linked work items:</strong> Ensures every commit is associated with a work item for tracking.</li>
                <li><strong>Enable build validation:</strong> Runs automated tests to ensure new changes do not introduce bugs.</li>
                <li><strong>Restrict who can push:</strong> Limits write access to specific users to prevent unauthorized changes.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Azure Repos provides **scalable, secure, and reliable** version control with built-in support for Git and TFVC. By leveraging **branching strategies, pull requests, and policies**, development teams can ensure efficient collaboration, maintain code integrity, and automate quality control measures.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Git vs TFVC** to choose the right version control system for your team.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-end mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("GitVsTFVC", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">Next: Git vs TFVC &rarr;</a>
    </div>
</div>
